{#
_sidebar_pane.html.twig — the desktop sidebar, the width it was dragged to, and
the boundary that drags it (#699).

One partial for the four pages that show a sidebar — the mailbox, Settings, the
calendar and the admin panel — where each used to carry its own
`<div class="hidden md:flex">` around the include. The width is a preference,
so it has to be the same number on all four: a sidebar that is 300px on the
mail and 224px in Settings reads as the page having moved rather than as a
preference being kept.

Below md there is no boundary and no handle: the sidebar is the drawer there,
and the burger is its control.

── The three widths a sidebar can have ──────────────────────────────────────

  dragged   --sidebar-width, this boundary's number, stored per user
  railed    3.5rem, from `.sidebar-rail` on <html> — the burger, above md
  peeking   --sidebar-peek-width, the longest row's, on hover, if switched on

The first two are layout and move the pane beside it. The third is not: the
aside leans out of .sidebar-pane over the mail and the wrapper keeps its width,
so nothing reflows while the pointer crosses the nav. See "The sidebar
boundary" in app.css, and ui--sidebar#_measureTruncation for the number.

Accepts:
  room     CSS selector for the pane the sidebar takes its room from, so the
           clamp stops before squeezing it below a phone's width. The mailbox
           passes its workspace (#755). The other pages
           mark their content pane `data-sidebar-room`, the default.
  surface  Whether to re-point the density tokens at the sidebar's surface
           (`data-surface` in app.css). Only the mailbox did, and this partial
           keeps it that way.
#}
{% set _room    = room|default('[data-sidebar-room]') %}
{% set _surface = surface|default(false) %}

<div class="sidebar-boundary hidden md:flex shrink-0"
     {% if noSidebar ?? false %}data-no-sidebar{% endif %}
     {% if _surface %}data-surface="sidebar"{% endif %}
     {% if app.user %}
     {# In the first paint rather than restored by JavaScript: a sidebar that
        appears at 224px and jumps to 300px once Stimulus connects is worse
        than one that does not remember at all. #}
     style="--sidebar-width: {{ app.user.sidebarWidth }}px"
     data-controller="ui--sidebar-split"
     {# The rail is the burger's, not the handle's: inert while it is on. #}
     data-ui--sidebar-split-variable-value="--sidebar-width"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-main-selector-value="{{ _room }}"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-inert-when-value=".sidebar-rail"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-state-url-value="{{ path('app_mail_sidebar_state') }}"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-token-value="{{ csrf_token('sidebar_state') }}"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-min-value="{{ constant('App\\Entity\\User\\User::SIDEBAR_MIN_WIDTH') }}"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-max-value="{{ constant('App\\Entity\\User\\User::SIDEBAR_MAX_WIDTH') }}"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-default-value="{{ constant('App\\Entity\\User\\User::SIDEBAR_DEFAULT_WIDTH') }}"
     {# The pane beside it is the app and the sidebar is furniture, so where
        both minimums cannot be met the content wins. A phone's width, like
        the workspace's dividers: the list has a stacked layout for
        exactly that size and dragging should be able to reach it. #}
     data-ui--sidebar-split-main-min-value="340"
     data-ui--sidebar-split-step-value="16"
     {% endif %}
>
    {# The box that carries the width — and the element the controller
       measures, which must be the same one: sizing a wrapper of pane + handle
       and measuring the pane lost the handle's pixels on every re-clamp.

       It is also the peek's trigger. The handle is NOT inside it, so reaching
       for the handle never opens the peek on the way. #}
    <div data-ui--sidebar-split-target="pane" class="sidebar-pane flex min-w-0">
        {% include template|default('_partials/_sidebar.html.twig') %}
    </div>

    {% if app.user %}
        {# Takes no room: app.css lays it absolutely over the gutter the row
           already leaves, exactly that wide, so it costs the layout nothing
           and covers neither card. Hidden while railed and while the peek is
           out. #}
        <div data-ui--sidebar-split-target="handle"
             data-sidebar-handle
             data-action="pointerdown->ui--sidebar-split#start keydown->ui--sidebar-split#nudge dblclick->ui--sidebar-split#reset"
             role="separator"
             aria-orientation="vertical"
             aria-label="{{ 'sidebar.resize'|trans }}"
             title="{{ 'sidebar.resize_hint'|trans }}"
             tabindex="0"
             class="group cursor-col-resize flex items-center justify-center focus:outline-none">
            {# `.split-grip`: the grip's colour transition (see "Workspaces"
               in app.css). #}
            <span class="split-grip w-0.5 h-8 rounded-full bg-line
                         group-hover:bg-accent group-focus-visible:bg-accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</div>
